$$
\mathbf { A _ { d } } = \kappa \cdot \left( \begin{array} { c c c c c c } { 0 } & { \cos ( \Delta \phi - \Omega ) } & { 0 } & { \ldots } & { 0 } & { \cos ( \Delta \phi + \Omega ) } \\ { \cos ( \Delta \phi + \Omega ) } & { 0 } & { \cos ( \Delta \phi - \Omega ) } & { \ldots } & { 0 } & { 0 } \\ { 0 } & { \cos ( \Delta \phi + \Omega ) } & { 0 } & { \ldots } & { 0 } & { 0 } \\ { \vdots } & { \vdots } & { \vdots } & { \ddots } & { \vdots } & { \vdots } \\ { 0 } & { 0 } & { 0 } & { \ldots } & { 0 } & { \cos ( \Delta \phi - \Omega ) } \\ { \cos ( \Delta \phi - \Omega ) } & { 0 } & { 0 } & { \ldots } & { \cos ( \Delta \phi + \Omega ) } & { 0 } \end{array} \right) ,
$$  

where $\mathbf{1}_{N}$ is the $N \times N$ identity matrix. Eq. (A.1) is a system of linear DDEs. For systems where the matrices of the delayed and non-delayed terms commute the solution is known [2]. In our case the commutation of $\mathbf{A}$ and $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{d}}$ is fulfilled since $\mathbf{A}$ is proportional to the identity matrix. The solution hence writes as:  

$$
\eta ( u ) = \sum _ { k = - \infty } ^ { + \infty } \mathrm { e } ^ { \left[ \mathbf { W } _ { k } ( \mathbf { A } _ { \mathrm { d } } \mathrm { e } ^ { - \mathbf { A } } ) + \mathbf { A } \right] u } \mathbf { c } _ { k } ^ { I } .
$$  

The matrix $\mathbf{W}_{k}$ is $k$-th branch of the matrix Lambert W function [23], while the vector $\mathbf{c}_{k}^{I}$ depends on the coefficient matrices, the initial conditions, and the history of the delayed system. Generally, in order to determine the stability we have to calculate the greatest eigenvalue of the exponent in square brackets over all $k$ values. Since the Lambert W function has infinite branches the whole eigenspectrum of the linear solution is infinite. For a scalar system, as opposed to Eq. (2), the principal branch of $k=0$ gives the rightmost eigenvalue. Fortunately for us the above statement holds for systems of DDEs as well if the coefficient matrices commute [2]. The term that contains the principal branch is the following:  

$$
\eta _ { 0 } ( u ) = \mathrm { e } ^ { [ \mathbf { W } _ { 0 } ( \mathbf { A } _ { \mathrm { d } } \mathrm { e } ^ { - \mathbf { A } } ) + \mathbf { A } ] u } \mathbf { c } _ { 0 } ^ { I } \, .
$$  

In order to easily calculate matrix exponentials we need to diagonalize the exponent. First, we have a matrix exponential in the argument of the Lambert W function, which is easy to calculate since $\mathbf{A}$ is diagonal:  

$$
\mathrm { e } ^ { - \mathbf { A } } = \mathrm { e } ^ { 2 \kappa \cos ( \Delta \phi ) \cos ( \Omega ) } \cdot \mathbf { 1 } _ { N } .
$$  

Thus the argument of the matrix Lambert W function is simplified, having only one matrix multiplied by a scalar  

$$
\mathbf { W } _ { 0 } ( \mathbf { A } _ { \mathrm { d } } \mathrm { e } ^ { - \mathbf { A } } ) = \mathbf { W } _ { 0 } ( \mathrm { e } ^ { 2 \kappa _ { c } } \mathbf { A } _ { \mathrm { d } } ) ,
$$  

where we introduced the shorthand notation $\kappa_{c}=\kappa\cos(\Delta\phi)\cos(\Omega)$. The $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{d}}$ matrix is circulant, thus diagonalizable using the unitary discrete Fourier transform matrix [6]: $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{d}}=\mathbf{U}\cdot\mathbf{D}\cdot\mathbf{U}^{-1}$. This property helps in evaluating the matrix Lambert W function, which is simply the scalar function acting on the diagonal elements  

$$
\begin{array} { r l r } & { } & { \mathbf { W } _ { 0 } ( \mathrm { e } ^ { 2 \kappa _ { c } } \mathbf { A _ { d } } ) = } \\ & { } & { \mathbf { U } \cdot \left( \begin{array} { c c c } { W _ { 0 } ( \mathrm { e } ^ { 2 \kappa _ { c } } \lambda ^ { 1 } ) } & { 0 } & { 0 } \\ { 0 } & { W _ { 0 } ( \mathrm { e } ^ { 2 \kappa _ { c } } \lambda ^ { 2 } ) } & { 0 } \\ { \vdots } & { \vdots } & { \vdots } \\ { 0 } & { 0 } & { W _ { 0 } ( \mathrm { e } ^ { 2 \kappa _ { c } } \lambda ^ { N } ) } \end{array} \right) \cdot \mathbf { U } ^ { - 1 } , } \end{array}
$$  

