图4上呈现了两个主要状态（$\Delta\phi=0$和$\Delta\phi=-\pi$）的分叉图。为了比较，我们还通过阴影区域表示了这些状态的概率。对于所选择的$\kappa$，无论$\omega$取值如何，这两个状态都至少有一个稳定的$\Omega$频率，请将此与图2进行比较。尽管存在多个稳定频率，但只有一个频率几乎作为渐近状态出现，考虑到上述初始化。有趣的是，正如图4所示，状态的概率与无量纲自然频率$\omega$和最终频率$\Omega$之间的差异强烈相关。换句话说，当$|\Omega_0-\omega|<|\Omega_{-\pi}-\omega|$且反之时，系统更倾向于稳定状态$\{\Delta\phi=0,\Omega_0\}$而不是稳定状态$\{\Delta\phi=-\pi,\Omega_{-\pi}\}$。此外，这种优势不仅是相对的，而且除了在接近$(2k+1)\frac{\pi}{2}$的狭窄$\omega$区间内，另一个状态根本不出现，其中前面提到的两个状态对$\omega$的距离几乎相同。在参考文献[9]中也报告了在具有高斯白噪声的延迟耦合振荡器环的情况下，停留在某一状态的时间与频率差异之间的类似相关性。换句话说，系统在频率最接近旋转体的固有频率的轨道上停留的时间最长。  

现在让我们考虑一些启发式的论据，关于实现与 $\Delta\phi=0$ 和 $\Delta\phi=-\pi$ 不同的其他稳定状态的问题。通过  

![](images/58de18b0348af4123d7d78499196536e829339b5690c1d3956fb10d2eb386ecd.jpg)  

Figure 4: Bifurcation diagram of the final frequencies for $m=0$ and $m=-5$ ($\Delta\phi=0$ and $\Delta\phi=-\pi$, respectively) as a function of the rescaled natural frequency $\omega/\pi$. On the vertical axis the difference between the final $\Omega$ and the natural $\omega$ frequencies is plotted, normalized with $2\kappa$. Stable/unstable frequencies are marked with solid/dashed lines. The black horizontal dashed line marks the $\Omega=\omega$ case. The width of orange and blue shadings correspond to the probability of observing the given frequency, compared to probability 1 indicated by gray shaded region. No shading at stable frequencies for any branch means zero observations. The other parameters are $N=10$, $\kappa=2$.  

1. Increasing $\kappa$ will make the slope of the linear function smaller in absolute value, thus there will be more intersections with the $\sin(\Omega)$ curve. For different $\Delta\phi$ values we will have lines with different slopes, all of them lying between the two extremes $\Delta\phi=0$ and $\Delta\phi=\pi$ and crossing each other at the $\Omega=\omega$ point. Modifying the $\omega$ is equivalent thus to sliding the linear function parallel to itself. Since the sine function is periodic, the number of solutions of Eq. (4) changes with the same $2\pi$ periodicity. Even though the number of possible solutions is known, their stability, apart of $\Delta\phi=\{0,-\pi\}$, is not yet determined. For the full bifurcation diagram we need again Eq. (A.13) for sorting out unstable states.  

The bifurcation diagram of the two dominant states ($\Delta\phi=0$ and $\Delta\phi=-\pi$) is presented on Fig. 4. For comparison we also indicate the probability of these states by shaded areas. For the chosen $\kappa$ both states have at least one stable $\Omega$ frequency for all $\omega$ values, please compare this with Fig. 2. Though there exist multiple stable frequencies, only one of them appears almost exclusively as asymptotic state, given the initialization described above. Interestingly, as Fig. 4 shows, the probability of the states strongly correlates with the difference between the dimensionless natural frequency $\omega$ and the final frequency $\Omega$. In other words, the system prefers a stable $\{\Delta\phi=0,\Omega_0\}$ state over an also stable $\{\Delta\phi=-\pi,\Omega_{-\pi}\}$ when $|\Omega_0-\omega|<|\Omega_{-\pi}-\omega|$ and vice versa. Furthermore, this dominance is not only relative, but the other state does not appear at all, except for narrow $\omega$ intervals around $(2k+1)\frac{\pi}{2}$, where the distances to $\omega$ are almost the same for both of the states mentioned before. A similar correlation between the resident time spent in one state and the difference in frequency is reported in Ref. [9] in the case of delay coupled rings of oscillators with Gaussian white noise. Namely, the system spends the most time on orbits which have the frequency closest to the natural frequency of the rotators.  

Let us consider now some heuristic arguments concerning the realization of other stable states different from $\Delta\phi=0$ and $\Delta\phi=-\pi$. By recalling Eq.(4) one can see that difference $\Omega-\omega$ is determined by  

$$
\delta = \cos ( \Delta \phi ) \sin ( \Omega ) \, ,
$$  

回忆方程（4），我们可以看到差异 $\Omega-\omega$ 是由以下决定的:  

$$
\delta = \cos ( \Delta \phi ) \sin ( \Omega ) \, ,
$$  

在这里，$-2\kappa$的因子被省略以保持该参数的标准化。使用与0和$-N/2$这两个特殊状态相同的$(\omega, \kappa)$参数来计算除$m$之外的其他状态的$\delta$，发现相对于这两个特殊状态，$\delta$可以更接近于0。另一方面，由于这些中间状态的概率往往较小（参见图??），在状态选择过程中必须有其他相关参数。可以注意到相对于$\Delta\phi=\{0, -\pi\}$情况，主要的区别与方程(2)中的两个正弦项的对称性有关，如果$\Delta\phi$既不是0也不是$-\pi$，那么它们会取不同的值。这种情况的一个结果是，在这样的状态中，振荡器会按照一个优选方向或顺序排列，因此，两个方向的相互作用强度的对称性被破坏。这主要是由于正弦是一个奇函数，因此它对振荡器的交换敏感，并且通过对其编号，我们已经赋予了一个预定义的方向，用于相位之间的比较。为了量化一个状态的对称性，我们引入了第二个参数。  

$$
\sigma = \sin ( \Delta \phi ) \cos ( \Omega ) \, ,
$$  

通过从给定的$\Omega$和$\Delta\phi$减去Eq.(2)中的两个正弦项来获得。类似于$\delta$，$2\kappa$项被省略。对称状态将具有$\sigma=0$。与图2中的稳定映射情况类似，具有$|m_{1}|+|m_{2}|=N/2$的态对在$\omega$变化时具有类似的$\delta$和$\sigma$趋势。此外，具有相反指数的态对($m_{1}=-m_{2}$)具有相同的$\delta$，但是$\sigma$相反。  

为了更好地理解$\delta$和$\sigma$之间的相互作用，我们在图5上绘制了Eq. (4)的稳定解在$\delta-\sigma$平面上的不同$\omega$值。根据这些图形，我们可以提出一些简单的启发式准则来理解振荡器是如何自组织的。  

- 首先，所选的状态倾向于尽可能对称，这就解释了为什么分布的峰值总是在0和$-\pi$左右。  

- 如果有不止一种与$|\sigma|$值相同的解，则首选$|\delta|$值较小的解（例如参见$\sigma=0$行）。  

- 具有相同的$|\sigma|$和$|\delta|$的状态具有相等的概率。  

基于以上观察，对于一个随机的初始态合集和给定的参数集，计算$|\sigma|$和$|\delta|$值可以帮助我们估计期望的吸引盆大小。  

最后, 请注意我们的系统是确定性的, 因此不需要基于概率的吸引盆大小方法, 然而时延存在将问题转化为无限维系统的范畴 [32], 这限制了动力学的可预测性。$N$ 维相空间也失去了它的传统意义, 因为$\theta_{i}$值不足以完全定义相空间中的状态, 状态历史也是必要的。  

# VI. CONCLUSIONS & OUTLOOK  

我们研究了通过时滞耦合相互作用的局部耦合Kuramoto振荡器环中的对称相位锁定状态。对称相位锁定状态是指相邻振荡器间允许有恒定的相位差。以往关于这样的局部耦合系统的研究只确定了有限数量的这种状态的稳定性标准。我们得到了所有具有恒定相位差状态的一种隐含稳定条件。为了了解稳定静止状态的选择统计情况，通过考虑大量均匀分布的随机初始状态，我们研究了其吸引盆地。我们的数值结果表明，吸引盆地的尺寸可以随参数变化几个数量级。这导致由于吸引域几乎可以忽略不计，从随机初始条件中稳定静止状态实际上是不可观测的。通过考虑简单的对称性论证，我们假设相对于吸引盆地的尺寸有启发式规则。在我们的数值研究中，与文献 [44] 的结果一致，我们还观察到时滞的存在通常会导致简单固定点吸引子的不稳定，从而除了这些相位锁定状态外，还出现了更复杂的动态行为。因此，在局部耦合的时滞系统中寻找不同类型的吸引子应该是未来研究的另一个目标。  

![](images/9fe1786126b1bae6d6fc93c58232fbb6c9cc732edad6b5c492612d3c41f538a0.jpg)  

![](images/7e8862e1b8d192c4ac0475bf806859e5bf73ba3a3436ed7d37d92944d8532820.jpg)  

Figure 5: Diagram of $\delta$ versus $\sigma$ for stable solutions of Eq. (4) calculated for three values of $\omega$. Dashed lines and curves represent the paths along which the $\Omega$ frequencies for different states move as $\omega$ changes. Numbers, corresponding to $|m|$, indicate the $\sigma$ and $\delta$ values determined using actual final frequencies for the $\omega$ noted above. The area of the gray circles are proportional to the probability of a given $\{\Delta\phi,\Omega\}$ pair. Note that in the middle plot the states denoted by numbers 5 and 0 correspond to slightly different $\delta$ values, hence leading to different probabilities (compare Fig. 3). The parameters are $N=10$, $\kappa=2.0$.  

where the $-2\kappa$ factor is dropped to keep this parameter normalized. Calculating $\delta$ using the same $(\omega, \kappa)$ parameters for other states $m$ than 0 and $-N/2$, one finds that $\delta$ can be closer to 0 than in the case of these two particular states. On the other hand, since the probability of these intermediate states tends to be smaller (see Fig. 3), there must be other relevant parameters as well in the process of state selection. One can notice that the main difference relative to the $\Delta\phi=\{0,-\pi\}$ case is related to the symmetry of the two sine terms in Eq. (2), taking different values if $\Delta\phi$ is neither 0, nor $-\pi$. One consequence of this is that in such states there is a preferred direction or order in which the oscillators arrange themselves, thus the symmetry of the interaction intensity for the two directions is broken. This is mainly caused by the fact that sine is an odd function, so it is sensible to the interchange of oscillators and by numbering them we already imposed a predefined direction in which the phases should be compared to each other. In order to quantify the symmetry of a state, we introduce a second parameter  

$$
\sigma = \sin ( \Delta \phi ) \cos ( \Omega ) \, ,
$$  

obtained by subtracting the two sine terms from Eq. (2) for a given $\Omega$ and $\Delta\phi$. Similarly to $\delta$ the $2\kappa$ term is omitted. Symmetric states will have $\sigma=0$. As in case of the stability maps in Fig. 2, pairs of states with $|m_1|+|m_2|=N/2$ have similar trends of $\delta$ and $\sigma$ as $\omega$ changes. Also, pairs with opposite index ($m_1=-m_2$) have the same $\delta$, but opposite $\sigma$.  

In order to better understand the interplay between $\delta$ and $\sigma$ we plotted on Fig. 5 the stable solutions of Eq. (4) on the $\delta - \sigma$ plane for different $\omega$ values. Based on these graphs we can formulate some simple heuristic guidelines along which one can understand how the oscillators self-organize themselves.  

- First, the selected state tends to be symmetric as possible, which explains why the peak of the distributions are always around 0 and $-\pi$.  

- If there is more than one solution with the same $|\sigma|$ value, the one with the smaller $|\delta|$ will be preferred (see for example the $\sigma=0$ line).  

- States, having the same $|\sigma|$ and $|\delta|$ are equally probable.  

